  "strategy": "We will use a genetic algorithm (GA) to search for the best normalized sequence (unit sum) of length 2048 that minimizes the evaluation function. The unprotected sequence length is fixed to 2048 to ensure sufficient complexity while respecting the time budget.\n\nThe algorithm consists of three phases:\n\n1. **Initialization**:\n   - Start with a population of 100 sequences of length 2048.\n   - Initialize sequences from several parameterized families, including:\n     - Uniform distribution: Each element is 1/2048.\n     - Exponential: Each element is proportional to exp(alpha * (position - center)).\n     - Two-level: A point separates two groups of elements, with values above and below.\n     - Quadratic bump: Follows a Gaussian-like profile.\n   - If `height_sequence_1` is available, incorporate it as one of the initial candidates.\n\n2. **Fitness Evaluation**:\n   - For each sequence, compute the objective using `evaluate_sequence`.\n   - Use tournament selection to choose parents: select 30 sequences based on their scores and pick the top 10 from that group for crossover.\n\n3. **Selection and Crossover**:\n   - For each generation, select two parent sequences from the best 10.\n   - Perform crossover by combining the two sequences: each element in the offspring is computed as (1 - alpha) * parent1[i] + alpha * parent2[i], where alpha is randomly distributed between 0 and 1.\n   - Apply mutation with a small probability (0.05) as local perturbations in contiguous regions.\n\nThe GA parameters are:\n\n   - Population size: 200 individuals (though only 100 are used per generation, the initialization phase uses a larger set).\n   - Tournament size: 30 individuals selected by their fitness scores.\n   - Crossover probability: 0.8\n   - Mutation probability: 0.05\n   - Maximum number of generations: 300 or until time exceeds.\n\nEach generation produces two offspring through crossover. The best individual is preserved to elude extinction. We also implement random restarts to maintain exploration if the population converges too quickly.\n\nThe search will run for up to 1800 seconds. Each sequence is normalized to have unit sum, so the evaluation function returns `2*n*max(conv)/sum^2`.\n\nAfter 1800 seconds, we return the best sequence found.\n\nLet's now set up the detailed implementation within the time budget.",
